(modifications, to each of them \(except Virginia, where he simply) Tj T*
(recognized the Pierpont government\).) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Before this policy was put into operation, however, an effort was made by) Tj T*
(some of the leaders of the Confederacy to secure the restoration of those) Tj T*
(states to the Union without the reconstruction and the pledge required by) Tj T*
(the President. After the surrender of Lee's army \(April 9, 1865\), General) Tj T*
(J. E. Johnston, acting under the authority of Jefferson Davis and with the) Tj T*
(advice of Breckenridge, the Confederate Secretary of War, and Reagan, the) Tj T*
(Confederate Postmaster General, proposed to General Sherman the surrender) Tj T*
(of all the Confederate armies then in existence on certain conditions.) Tj T*
(Among these was the condition that the executive of the United States) Tj T*
(should recognize the lately hostile state governments upon the renewal by) Tj T*
(their officers of their oath of allegiance to the federal Constitution,) Tj T*
(and that the people of the states so recognized should be guaranteed, so) Tj T*
(far as this lay in the power of the executive, their political rights as) Tj T*
(defined by the federal Constitution. Sherman signed a convention with) Tj T*
(Johnston agreeing to these terms, on April 18. That he intended by the) Tj T*
(agreement to commit the federal government to any permanent policy is) Tj T*
(doubtful. But when the convention was communicated for ratification to his) Tj T*
(superiors at headquarters, they showed the most decided opposition to) Tj T*
(granting the terms proposed even temporarily. The convention was) Tj T*
(emphatically disavowed, and on April 26 Sherman had to content himself) Tj T*
(with the surrender of Johnston's army only, agreed to on purely military) Tj T*
(terms.[1]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Georgia formed a part of the district under the command of General) Tj T*
(Johnston. As soon, therefore, as the news of the surrender could reach) Tj T*
(that state, hostilities there ceased. On May 3, Governor Brown issued a) Tj T*
(summons for a meeting of the state legislature to take place on May 22, in) Tj T*
(order that measures might be taken "to prevent anarchy, restore and) Tj T*
(preserve order, and save what [could be saved] of liberty and) Tj T*
(civilization."[2] At a time of general consternation, when military) Tj T*
(operations had displaced local government and closed the courts in many) Tj T*
(places, when the whole population was in want[3] through the devastation) Tj T*
(of the war or through the collapse of the Confederate currency which) Tj T*
(followed the collapse of the Confederate army,[4] the need of such) Tj T*
(measures was apparent.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The calling of the legislature incurred the disapproval of the federal) Tj T*
(authorities for two reasons. First, they regarded it as an attempt to) Tj T*
(prepare for further hostilities, and they accordingly arrested Brown,) Tj T*
(carried him to Washington, and put him in prison.[5] Second, in any case,) Tj T*
(as the disavowal of the convention of April 18 had shown, they did not) Tj T*
(intend to allow the state governments of the South to resume their regular) Tj T*
(activities at once, and accordingly the commander of the Department of the) Tj T*
(South issued orders on May 15, declaring void the proclamation of Joseph) Tj T*
(E. Brown, "styling himself Governor of Georgia," and forbidding obedience) Tj T*
(thereto.[6]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The federal army now took control of the entire state government.) Tj T*
(Detachments were stationed in all the principal towns and county seats,) Tj T*
