() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER II) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(THE JOHNSON GOVERNMENT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(From the conduct of the state governments formed in Georgia and the other) Tj T*
(southern states under the direction of President Johnson, the public) Tj T*
(opinion of the North drew conclusions regarding three things; the) Tj T*
(disposition of the people represented by those governments toward the) Tj T*
(emancipated slaves, their attitude toward the cause for which they had) Tj T*
(fought, and their feeling toward the power which had subdued them. This) Tj T*
(chapter treats the Johnson government of Georgia from the same points of) Tj T*
(view.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Whatever may have been the prevailing disposition of the white people) Tj T*
(toward the slaves while slavery flourished, shortly before the close of) Tj T*
(the war that disposition was characterized by benevolence and gratitude.) Tj T*
(In spite of the opportunities of escape, and of plunder and other) Tj T*
(violence, offered by the times, the slaves had acted with singular) Tj T*
(faithfulness and devotion.[27] The gratitude of their masters even went so) Tj T*
(far as to propose plans for the general education of the negroes.[28]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The close of the war and the advent of emancipation produced a change in) Tj T*
(the conduct of the negroes, which in time produced a change in the) Tj T*
(attitude of the white people. The negroes, from the talk which they heard) Tj T*
(and did not understand, and from their ignorant imaginations, conceived) Tj T*
(strange ideas of emancipation. They supposed it meant governmental) Tj T*
(bounty, idleness, and wealth. They abandoned their work, wandered about) Tj T*
(the country, collected in towns--in short, manifested a general) Tj T*
(restlessness and demoralization. This caused alarm and apprehension among) Tj T*
(the white people. There were other causes of friction between the two) Tj T*
(races. Many negroes, on discovering that they were free, assumed what are) Tj T*
(known as "airs;" and then as now, among things intolerable to a southern) Tj T*
(white man a "sassy nigger" held a curious pre-eminence. The airs of the) Tj T*
(negro and the wrath of the white man were both augmented by officious) Tj T*
(members of the Freedmen's Bureau. Moreover, because the negroes had gained) Tj T*
(by the humiliation of the South, they received a share of the venom of) Tj T*
(defeat. Another element of discord was furnished by a particular part of) Tj T*
(the white population, the so-called poor whites. These saw in the new) Tj T*
(_proteges_ of the United States not only a rival laboring class, but a) Tj T*
(menace to their social position, and hence assumed an attitude of jealousy) Tj T*
(and hatred. Such were the conditions favorable to social disturbance which) Tj T*
(followed emancipation. In the latter part of 1865 they had already begun) Tj T*
(to produce their natural result. Violent encounters between negroes and) Tj T*
(white men \(in which the latter were almost always the aggressors\) were) Tj T*
(noticeably frequent.[29]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To this social demoralization was added economic distress and perplexity.) Tj T*
ET
endstream
