(anti-slavery agitation, and now intensified by the passions of the war. In) Tj T*
(such an hour a plan which frankly denied to the negroes political and) Tj T*
(social equality was looked upon as an offence against justice and) Tj T*
(humanity. The Georgia law-makers had sought for a plan to meet immediate) Tj T*
(necessities, not a plan for the elevation of the black race. To demand) Tj T*
(that Georgia, stricken and menaced as she was, should pass by the needs of) Tj T*
(the present and enter upon a vague scheme of philanthropy, was) Tj T*
(unreasonable. It was just as unreasonable to conclude from the course) Tj T*
(which Georgia took, that the black race in Georgia would be forever held) Tj T*
(down, or that positive encouragement would be withheld as time went on.) Tj T*
(Nevertheless the public opinion of the North made this demand and drew) Tj T*
(these conclusions.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
