() Tj T*
(3. The convention anticipated the function of the legislature in order to) Tj T*
(provide pensions for the wounded Confederate soldiers and for widows of) Tj T*
(the dead.[47]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Through the legislature Georgia showed herself equally frank in expressing) Tj T*
(affection and regret for the lost cause, and equally wanting in an) Tj T*
(attitude of humility to the federal government--or at least to the) Tj T*
(dominant party in Congress. On the recommendation of the governor she) Tj T*
(rejected the Fourteenth Amendment by an almost unanimous vote, largely) Tj T*
(because of the disabilities it imposed on the leaders of the) Tj T*
(Confederacy.[48] Instead of remaining a humbly silent spectator of the) Tj T*
(controversy between the President and Congress, she boldly thanked the) Tj T*
(President for his "regard for the constitutional rights of states," and) Tj T*
(for "the determined will that says to a still hostile faction of her) Tj T*
(recent foes, 'Thus far shalt thou go and no farther. Peace, be) Tj T*
