(excluded from office by the pending Fourteenth Amendment. These were) Tj T*
(forbidden to sit in the convention or to vote for delegates. The) Tj T*
(convention thus formed should frame a new constitution, which should give) Tj T*
(the franchise to all persons qualified to vote for delegates by the) Tj T*
(present bill. The constitution should be submitted to the people of the) Tj T*
(state for ratification, and to Congress for approval. When these should) Tj T*
(have been received, and when the legislature elected under the new) Tj T*
(constitution should have ratified the Fourteenth Amendment, then Congress) Tj T*
(should pass an act admitting the reconstructed state to Congressional) Tj T*
(representation, and the present law should cease to operate in that) Tj T*
(state.[69]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The principle of this bill was the same as that of the reconstruction) Tj T*
(measures first undertaken at the suggestion of the joint committee, namely) Tj T*
(the punishment of an enemy. The debate in the House was opened by a) Tj T*
(felicitous quotation from Vattel on the public law applicable to the case) Tj T*
(of a conquered enemy.[70] The punishment here provided was, however, more) Tj T*
(severe than that first proposed. The former program was designed to offer) Tj T*
(to the states the alternative of adopting the Fourteenth Amendment or) Tj T*
(remaining out of the Union and under the Freedman's Bureau--which was,) Tj T*
(indeed, regarded as a very obnoxious alternative. But the present bill) Tj T*
(required them not only to ratify the amendment, but to adopt new) Tj T*
(constitutions, elect new governments, enfranchise the negroes, and) Tj T*
(disfranchise their most prominent and respected citizens; and meanwhile) Tj T*
(imposed upon them not simply a bureau, to interfere in individual cases,) Tj T*
(but the virtually absolute rule of a military governor.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This bill was passed over Johnson's veto on March 2, 1867. On March 23 a) Tj T*
(supplementary act was passed, providing means for executing section 5 of) Tj T*
(the preceding act. The initiative in calling the constitutional) Tj T*
(conventions, instead of being left to the states, to be exercised or not,) Tj T*
(as they chose, was now assigned to the military governor. He, with the) Tj T*
(assistance of such boards of registry as he might create, was directed to) Tj T*
(register all persons qualified to vote for delegates. He should then fix) Tj T*
(the number of delegates and arrange the plan of representation, set the) Tj T*
(day for election and summon the convention.[71]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A third reconstruction act was passed on July 19, 1867. It is unnecessary) Tj T*
(to discuss it, since it was only explanatory of the acts of March 2 and) Tj T*
(23, and added nothing which needs mention here to their provisions.[72]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(       *       *       *       *       *) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Were the Reconstruction Acts constitutional? Since the Supreme Court has) Tj T*
(failed, either voluntarily or otherwise, to decide every case brought) Tj T*
(before it depending upon this question,[73] reasoning is not rendered idle) Tj T*
(by authority. The Supreme Court has indeed expressed a definite opinion on) Tj T*
(the subject, but has given no decision.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
