(     These new relations [namely, those created by the civil war] imposed) Tj T*
(     new duties upon the United States. The first was that of suppressing) Tj T*
(     the rebellion. The next was that of re-establishing the broken) Tj T*
(     relations of the states with the Union. The authority for the) Tj T*
(     performance of the first had been found in the power to suppress) Tj T*
(     insurrection and to carry on war; for the performance of the second,) Tj T*
(     authority was derived from the obligation of the United States to) Tj T*
(     guarantee to every state a republican form of government.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This the Court considered good authority for the passage of the) Tj T*
(Reconstruction Acts. Most of the advocates of the acts based them upon) Tj T*
(this theory.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Now, upon that clause of Article IV., Section 4, of the Constitution which) Tj T*
(says: "The United States shall guarantee to every state in this Union a) Tj T*
(republican form of government," the _Federalist_ remarks:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     It may possibly be asked whether [this clause] may not become a) Tj T*
(     pretext for alterations in the state governments without the) Tj T*
(     concurrence of the states themselves.... But the authority extends no) Tj T*
(     further than to a _guarantee_ [the _Federalist's_ italics] of a) Tj T*
(     republican form of government, which supposes a pre-existing) Tj T*
(     government of the form which is to be guaranteed.[75]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The intention of the clause, says the _Federalist_ in the same paper, is) Tj T*
(simply to guard "against aristocratic or monarchic innovations." To one) Tj T*
(not interested in establishing the constitutionality of the Reconstruction) Tj T*
(Acts, it seems indisputable that the clause is rightly interpreted by the) Tj T*
(_Federalist_. Story accepts this interpretation as a matter of course.[76]) Tj T*
(Cooley groups the clause with that which forbids the states to grant) Tj T*
(titles of nobility.[77] If this interpretation is correct, then the) Tj T*
(guarantee clause gives no authority for destroying a state government of a) Tj T*
(republican form and substituting another.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(There is, however, a constitutional basis for the Reconstruction Acts. It) Tj T*
(is the war power of Congress.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If a section of the people of a stale rebel against the government, the) Tj T*
(resulting contest is not a war, in the sense of international law. But as) Tj T*
(it may assume the physical character of a war, so it may call into) Tj T*
(existence the rights and customs incident to war. Upon this principle the) Tj T*
(federal government acquired the rights of war in the contest of) Tj T*
(1861-1865.[78] Now the rights of war do not end with military operations;) Tj T*
(one of these rights is the right of the victorious party, after an) Tj T*
(unconditional surrender, to occupy the territory of the defeated party, to) Tj T*
(govern or punish the people as it sees fit. If the United States) Tj T*
(government acquired the rights of war, this right was included. The close) Tj T*
(of a war is not simultaneous with the cessation of fighting. The surrender) Tj T*
(of the southern armies was an important incident in the civil war; it was) Tj T*
(not the end. If the federal government had the rights of war before this) Tj T*
(incident, it had them after.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
ET
