(The United States government might therefore say to the persons composing) Tj T*
(the military power which it had subdued: As the terms of war, you are to) Tj T*
(be governed by military government. If the persons against whom this) Tj T*
(sentence is assumed to have been pronounced formed the majority of the) Tj T*
(population of a state, one result of the sentence would be to suspend) Tj T*
(independent state government. The United States government might choose) Tj T*
(another punishment. It might say to the lately hostile persons: We forbid) Tj T*
(you to participate in the federal government. If the persons so sentenced) Tj T*
(form the majority of the population of a state, that state can send no) Tj T*
(representatives to Congress while the sentence remains. These sentences) Tj T*
(might be imposed permanently or only until such time as the people) Tj T*
(sentenced should fulfil certain demands--hold certain conventions, pass) Tj T*
(certain laws, adopt certain resolutions in certain ways. The federal) Tj T*
(government can thus effect through its war powers what it cannot effect) Tj T*
(through any power to interfere directly with a state government. It had no) Tj T*
(right to reconstruct the government of Maine in 1865, because Maine had no) Tj T*
(body of people over whom the federal government could exercise war powers.) Tj T*
(It had the right to reconstruct the government of Georgia, because) Tj T*
(nine-tenths of the people of Georgia were lawfully at its mercy as a) Tj T*
(conqueror.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Even if it be admitted, however, that the federal government had the power) Tj T*
(described, it may still be argued that the Reconstruction Acts are not) Tj T*
(legally justified. A conqueror has a right to govern a conquered people as) Tj T*
(he pleases and as long as he pleases; he also has a right to alter his) Tj T*
(mode of treatment and substitute another mode. But after he has imposed) Tj T*
(certain terms as final, after the requirements of these terms have been) Tj T*
(complied with, after he has restored the conquered people to their normal) Tj T*
(position and rights and has unmistakably terminated the relation of) Tj T*
(conqueror to conquered--then his rights of war are at an end. It may be) Tj T*
(argued that this was the case when the Reconstruction Acts were passed. It) Tj T*
(may be argued that in December, 1865, the federal government had, through) Tj T*
(the President, terminated its capacity as a conqueror, and could regain) Tj T*
(that capacity only by another war; that after that termination it had no) Tj T*
(more power to reconstruct Georgia than to reconstruct Maine.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(This argument is irrefutable if we assume that the President had full) Tj T*
(power to act for the federal government in the disposition of the defeated) Tj T*
(Secessionists, and that therefore his acts of 1865 were the acts of the) Tj T*
(federal government. In case of an international war, which is closed by a) Tj T*
(treaty, the President may \(if supported by the Senate\) act finally for the) Tj T*
(federal government, and estop that government \(so far as international law) Tj T*
(is concerned\) from further action. But at the close of a civil war he) Tj T*
(cannot exercise his diplomatic power. The disposition of the defeated) Tj T*
(people in this case falls to the legislative branch of the government.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(If the President had pardoned a great majority of the Secessionists, that) Tj T*
(fact perhaps might have legally estopped Congress from passing the) Tj T*
(Reconstruction Acts. These acts were a war punishment, and a pardon cuts) Tj T*
(off further punishment.[79] But the total number of persons who received) Tj T*
(amnesty under the proclamation of May 29, 1865, was 13,596,[80] which was) Tj T*
(of course only a small fraction of the Secessionist population.) Tj T*
