() Tj T*
(The passage of the Reconstruction Acts may thus be regarded, from a legal) Tj T*
(point of view, as simply the substitution of one method of treating the) Tj T*
(defeated enemy for another. The change was from mildness to harshness. It) Tj T*
(was doubly bitter to the defeated enemy, after he had been led to believe) Tj T*
(that his punishment was over, to be subjected to a worse one. But these) Tj T*
(are not legal considerations.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(That the Reconstruction Acts required communities not states to ratify a) Tj T*
(constitutional amendment did not affect their legality. That an amendment) Tj T*
(depended for its validity on such ratification might make the amendment) Tj T*
(void \(though even from this result there is a means of escape in the) Tj T*
(theory of relation, to be mentioned later\), but that would not affect the) Tj T*
(act requiring the ratification. That this requirement was not made with) Tj T*
(the exclusive purpose of obtaining votes for the passage of the amendment) Tj T*
(is shown by a resolution introduced into the House of Representatives on) Tj T*
(July 21, 1867, which reads:) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(     _Resolved_, That in ratifying amendments to the Constitution of the) Tj T*
(     United States ... the said several states ... are wholly incapable) Tj T*
(     either of accepting or rejecting any such amendment so as to bind the) Tj T*
(     loyal states of the Union, ... and that when any amendment ... shall) Tj T*
(     be adopted by three-fourths of the states recognized by the Congress) Tj T*
(     as lawfully entitled to do so, ... the same shall become thereby a) Tj T*
(     part of the Constitution.[81]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(What virtues the Reconstruction Acts had besides legal regularity will be) Tj T*
(discussed later.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
