(ordered that thereafter the state's patronage should be given only to) Tj T*
(loyal papers.[103] Another measure to the same end was the order that no) Tj T*
(state court should entertain any action against any person for any acts) Tj T*
(done under the military authority.[104] But while opposition by state) Tj T*
(officers was thus dealt with, freedom of public opinion was emphatically) Tj T*
(declared. The declaration accompanied a public reprimand administered to) Tj T*
(the post commander at Mobile for interference with a newspaper.[105]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The careful consideration for the needs of the freedmen shown in the) Tj T*
(general's method of forming the boards of registry, in his instructions to) Tj T*
(the registrars, in his provision of certificates of registration to) Tj T*
(migrating citizens, and in his extension of the time of election, has been) Tj T*
(pointed out. Of a similar character was the warning to employers that any) Tj T*
(attempt to prevent laborers from voting, or to influence their votes by) Tj T*
(docking wages, threats, or any other means, would be severely dealt) Tj T*
(with.[106]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In his first general orders, as we have said, Pope warned the judiciary) Tj T*
(against racial prejudice. It was probably disregard of this warning which) Tj T*
(caused the removal of about a dozen judges, justices of the peace, and) Tj T*
(sheriffs.[107] In the interest of equal justice, Pope also ordered that) Tj T*
(grand and petit jurors should be selected impartially from the lists of) Tj T*
(voters registered under the Reconstruction Acts.[108] Besides this general) Tj T*
(protection, individual relief was given by release from arrest, mitigation) Tj T*
(of the conditions of confinement, reduction of fines, and other special) Tj T*
(dispensations.[109] The method of securing justice mentioned in the Act of) Tj T*
(March 2, 1867, namely by ordering the trial of cases by military) Tj T*
(commissions, was employed by Pope only once.[110]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Such was the administration of Pope. Its influence on the _personnel_ of) Tj T*
(the state government was large, but was exercised only slightly through) Tj T*
(removal, chiefly through appointment to fill vacancies. Pope removed about) Tj T*
(fifteen state officers \(almost all of whom were the judicial officers) Tj T*
(mentioned in the preceding paragraph\). He filled about two hundred) Tj T*
(vacancies.[111] It is significant that a great number of these were caused) Tj T*
(by resignation. His acts of interference with the action of state officers) Tj T*
(were few, and with all his zeal for the success of reconstruction, he) Tj T*
(favored freedom of speech. Nevertheless, his opinions and his personal) Tj T*
(character, combined with such interference as he did practice, served to) Tj T*
(gain for him the dislike of the people and the rather unjust reputation of) Tj T*
(a petty tyrant.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Though Meade lacked Pope's zealous enthusiasm for reconstruction, yet he) Tj T*
