(citizens of equal rights with all other citizens by the new) Tj T*
(constitution.[164] Therefore they had the right to hold office. It was) Tj T*
(true that the constitution did not grant the right to hold office to the) Tj T*
(negroes expressly, as it granted the right to vote; but in view of the) Tj T*
(fact that the convention which made the constitution was elected by 25,000) Tj T*
(white and 85,000 colored men, and that that constitution was adopted by) Tj T*
(35,000 white and 70,000 colored men, it would be absurd to suppose that) Tj T*
(the intent of that instrument was to withhold office from the) Tj T*
(negroes.[165] On the other side, it was argued that the right to hold) Tj T*
(office did not belong to every citizen, but only to such citizens as the) Tj T*
(law specially designated, or to such as possessed it by common law or) Tj T*
(custom. Irwin's Code could not be cited to prove that negroes had the) Tj T*
