(Little need be said of the manner in which this bill was passed. The) Tj T*
(usual partisan abuse prevailed on both sides. The Democrats made a) Tj T*
(remarkable opposition, led by Beck of Kentucky.[198] The Republicans were) Tj T*
(aided by a message from President Grant urging the intervention of) Tj T*
(Congress,[199] by the report of the reconstruction committee on affairs in) Tj T*
(Georgia,[200] and by a report from General Terry, who was stationed in the) Tj T*
(Department of the South, alleging that disorder was rampant in Georgia and) Tj T*
(the need of further military government by federal authority) Tj T*
(imperative.[201] Terry's superior officer, General Halleck, added a) Tj T*
(postscript to Terry's report to the effect that Terry was mistaken, that) Tj T*
(the disorder in Georgia was much less than was commonly believed, and that) Tj T*
(federal interference was highly inadvisable.[202] Aided by the report and) Tj T*
(undeterred by the postscript, the Republicans discoursed of "rebel) Tj T*
(control" and "murder" with unprecedented effect. Butler said that Congress) Tj T*
(must act instantly; if action on the bill is postponed, he said, "the rest) Tj T*
(of the Republican majority of that state may be murdered, even during) Tj T*
(Christmas week, when the Son of God came on earth to bring peace and good) Tj T*
(will to man."[203]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The bill became law on December 22, 1869.[204] Congress thus decided at) Tj T*
(last to adopt the opinion of the Senate judiciary committee, that Georgia) Tj T*
(had not become a state through the Omnibus Act. General Meade, in) Tj T*
(declaring the contrary, had been mistaken. Bullock, in calling himself) Tj T*
(governor, had been mistaken. The House of Representatives, in admitting) Tj T*
(members sent from Georgia, had been mistaken; they were _de facto_) Tj T*
(members, but had no legal right there.[205] The legal basis of the act of) Tj T*
(December 22 was then the same as that of the original Reconstruction Acts.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The question which had been raised in the debates on these acts--What) Tj T*
(legal effect could the action of a body not the legislature of a state) Tj T*
(have on the adoption of an amendment to the constitution?--was raised) Tj T*
(again here. Some of the Republicans argued that such action could have no) Tj T*
(effect and should not be required.[206] Under these circumstances there) Tj T*
(was a more earnest effort than any heretofore made to defend such a) Tj T*
(requirement. It was answered: True, the body which will ratify the) Tj T*
(amendment in Georgia will not be a state legislature at the time; but it) Tj T*
(will later become a state legislature, and then by relation the) Tj T*
(ratification will be imputed to the state legislature and will thus have) Tj T*
(legal effect. Relation, an operation known to private law, had been) Tj T*
(applied to constitutional law in several previous cases, in order to give) Tj T*
(to acts done by the legislatures of territories the same effect as if they) Tj T*
(had been done after statehood was obtained.[207] The ratification by) Tj T*
(Georgia would be valid by relation.[208]) Tj T*
