() Tj T*
(CHAPTER IX) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(RECONSTRUCTION AND THE STATE GOVERNMENT) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the preceding chapters we have mentioned the immediate effect of) Tj T*
(reconstruction upon social conditions. To its immediate effects upon) Tj T*
(political conditions, in other words to the character and conduct of the) Tj T*
(new state government, which have been mentioned only incidentally, we) Tj T*
(shall now give a more direct and consecutive consideration.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(With reference to the political reforms of reconstruction the white men of) Tj T*
(Georgia formed three distinct parties. There were those who favored them,) Tj T*
(either on their ethical and political merits or \(more often\) as a means of) Tj T*
(attaining political power otherwise unattainable. They were called) Tj T*
(Scalawags, Carpet-baggers and Radicals, of which terms we shall adopt the) Tj T*
(last. There were those unalterably opposed to them, called Rebels by their) Tj T*
(critics and Conservatives by themselves. There were, thirdly, those who) Tj T*
(supported them not upon their merits, which they doubted, but because they) Tj T*
(saw the state at the mercy of a conqueror and believed that, bad as the) Tj T*
(measures were, it was better to accept them quickly than to make a vain) Tj T*
(resistance, which could only prolong the social and commercial) Tj T*
(disturbances in the state, and which might occasion the administration of) Tj T*
(a still worse dose. This group embraced many of the commercial class,) Tj T*
(which was especially large in Georgia, and one of the men prominent in) Tj T*
(former politics, namely Governor Brown. They were classed by the) Tj T*
(Conservatives with the basest of Radicals, but we shall call them the) Tj T*
(Moderate Republicans. The admixture of this group with the Radical party) Tj T*
(had important consequences. Differing from their party in principle and) Tj T*
(allying themselves with it to bring peace to the state, when the peace of) Tj T*
(the state seemed secure, they sometimes adhered to their principles rather) Tj T*
(than to their party. It is true, many of them became so interested in the) Tj T*
(great game of politics then going on that they played it for its own sake,) Tj T*
(but some party splits of importance occurred.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The first fruit of the policy of negro enfranchisement and rebel) Tj T*
(disenfranchisement was the constitutional convention of 1867-68. It was) Tj T*
(stated in the latter part of Chapter IV. that in the election for members) Tj T*
(of this convention many Conservatives declined to take part. For this) Tj T*
(reason the Radicals obtained a predominance in the convention which they) Tj T*
