(bill was passed over his veto.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although Republican power was now doomed in a few weeks, and although) Tj T*
(resistance to a legislature which could easily override his vetoes was) Tj T*
(futile, yet Conley stubbornly continued to offer obstructions to the) Tj T*
(legislature at every possible point up to the very day when his successor) Tj T*
(was inaugurated.[321] He exhibited a courage and a political efficiency) Tj T*
(worthy of his predecessor, but accomplished nothing. He was able, however,) Tj T*
(to help his friends by means of the pardoning power. Several prominent) Tj T*
(Republicans were indicted at this time for various acts of public) Tj T*
(malfeasance. On the ground that in the existing state of public excitement) Tj T*
(these men could not obtain a fair trial, Conley ordered proceedings) Tj T*
(against several of these to be discontinued.[322]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On January 11, 1872, the returns from the special election were sent to) Tj T*
(the legislature by Conley, under protest,[323] and James M. Smith was) Tj T*
(declared elected. On January 12, Smith was inaugurated. Conley assisted at) Tj T*
(this ceremony, thus yielding the last inch of Republican ground.[324]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Reviewing the events recorded from the beginning of this chapter, we) Tj T*
(observe that the period of reconstruction in Georgia was not a period when) Tj T*
(a swarm of harpies took possession of the state government and preyed at) Tj T*
(will upon a helpless people. The constitutional convention of 1867-68) Tj T*
(forebodes such a period, but when the Conservatives rouse themselves, from) Tj T*
(that time on the stage presents an internecine war between two very well) Tj T*
(matched enemies. This struggle is usually represented as between a wicked) Tj T*
(assailant and a righteous assailed. That it was a struggle between) Tj T*
(Republicans and Democrats is much more characteristic. In such a contest) Tj T*
(mutual vilifying of course abounded, and it is not to be supposed _a) Tj T*
(priori_ that the vilifying of one party was more truthful than that of the) Tj T*
(other.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It is often vaguely said that reconstruction resulted in government by) Tj T*
(carpet-baggers. John B. Gordon, the Conservative candidate for governor) Tj T*
