(The reconstruction government is charged with having imposed such heavy) Tj T*
(taxes that as a result the people were impoverished, industry was checked,) Tj T*
(and many plantations went to waste. During the decade before the war the) Tj T*
(law provided that a tax should be annually levied at such a rate as to) Tj T*
(produce $375,000, provided the rate should not exceed one-twelfth of one) Tj T*
(per cent.[326] The revenue law of 1866 provided that a tax should be) Tj T*
(levied at such a rate as to produce $350,000.[327] Owing to the vast) Tj T*
(destruction of property during the war, this necessitated a higher rate) Tj T*
(than that before the war. The law of 1867 ordered a levy at such a rate as) Tj T*
(to raise $500,000.[328] This law, made by the Johnson government, before) Tj T*
(reconstruction began, was continued by the legislature in the four) Tj T*
(following years.[329] In 1870 the rate of assessment was two-fifths of one) Tj T*
(per cent.[330] This rate was much higher than the one prevailing before) Tj T*
(the war, but this misfortune cannot be charged to reconstruction, since) Tj T*
(the reconstruction government merely followed the example of the Johnson) Tj T*
(government.) Tj T*
