(The state debt created by the reconstruction government was of two kinds;) Tj T*
(direct and contingent. When the reconstruction government went into) Tj T*
(operation the state debt was $6,544,500.[341] The reconstruction) Tj T*
(government incurred a bonded debt of $4,880,000.[342] This includes bonds) Tj T*
(to the amount of $1,880,000 which were issued to a railroad in exchange) Tj T*
(for its bonds to a greater amount and bearing interest at the same rate.) Tj T*
(This amount, therefore, was not a burden on the state, provided the) Tj T*
(railroad remained solvent; though in form a direct, it was virtually a) Tj T*
(contingent liability. Further, $300,000 of the money borrowed was used to) Tj T*
(pay the principal of the old debt. Deducting these two sums, we find that) Tj T*
(the burden of direct debt was increased by $2,700,000.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Contingent debt was incurred by the indorsement of railroad bonds. In 1868) Tj T*
(the state offered aid of this kind to three railroad companies,[343] in) Tj T*
(1869 to four,[344] and in 1870 to thirty.[345] The state offered to) Tj T*
(indorse the bonds of each of these companies to the amount, usually, of) Tj T*
(from $12,000 to $15,000 per mile, sometimes more and sometimes less. If) Tj T*
(all the roads had accepted the full amount of aid offered, the state would) Tj T*
(have become contingently liable for about $30,000,000.[346] But only six) Tj T*
(roads accepted, and the contingent liability thus created was) Tj T*
($6,923,400.[347] The laws offering the aid involved little risk to the) Tj T*
(state; they made substantial progress in construction and substantial) Tj T*
(evidence of soundness conditions precedent to indorsement, and secured to) Tj T*
(the state a lien on all the property of each road in case it defaulted.) Tj T*
(The indorsement of railroad bonds is not a reproach to the reconstruction) Tj T*
(government. The great policy of that government, when it was sufficiently) Tj T*
(free from partisan labors to have a policy, was to repair the prosperity) Tj T*
(of the state, and the construction of railroads was an important means to) Tj T*
(this end.[348]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The worst stain on the reconstruction government is its management of the) Tj T*
(state railroad. The Western and Atlantic Railroad, owned and operated by) Tj T*
(the state until 1871, was placed under the superintendence of Foster) Tj T*
(Blodgett by the governor in January, 1870.[349] Thenceforth hundreds of) Tj T*
(employees were discharged to make room for Republican favorites; important) Tj T*
