(positions were filled by strangers to the business; the receipts were) Tj T*
(stolen,[350] or squandered in purchases made from other Republicans at) Tj T*
(monstrous prices; and the road suffered great dilapidation.[351]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The preferred object of the Conservative abuse in the reconstruction) Tj T*
(government was Governor Bullock. We have seen that he was remarkably) Tj T*
(powerful as well as remarkably active in promoting the interests of his) Tj T*
(party. He was abused for that. For the extravagance of the state) Tj T*
(government the governor was held largely responsible. He was abused for) Tj T*
(that. But he was further accused of fraud in financial matters.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Although this charge has never been established, the public had some) Tj T*
(excuse for believing it at the time. As a result of the quarrel between) Tj T*
(the governor and the treasurer, the governor ordered the bankers who were) Tj T*
(the financial agents of the state to hold no further communication with) Tj T*
(the treasurer after June 3, 1869, but to communicate only with the) Tj T*
(governor.[352] The effect upon the public was an impression of great) Tj T*
(confusion and irregularity in the finances. The treasurer's reports could) Tj T*
(not give a complete account of state moneys, and the governor was not) Tj T*
(careful to inform the public of the condition of that part of the finances) Tj T*
(over which he had assumed control. Moreover, the governor and the) Tj T*
(treasurer kept up a constant interchange of accusation and insinuation in) Tj T*
(the newspapers. In another way the governor put himself in an unfortunate) Tj T*
(light. In his letter to the Ku Klux Committee his statements regarding his) Tj T*
(bond transactions were so vague as to give the impression \(rightly or) Tj T*
(wrongly\) of a desire to conceal something.[353] The same laxity of) Tj T*
(statement appears in Conley's statement of the use to which the bonds) Tj T*
(issued by Bullock had been put.[354] His sudden resignation and departure) Tj T*
(on the eve of a threatened investigation seemed to confirm the evidence of) Tj T*
(his guilt.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(But though he did not keep the public informed, it has never been) Tj T*
(established that his accounts were wrong. He spent money freely, and in) Tj T*
(some cases without authority;[355] but none of his accusers has ever) Tj T*
(proved that he spent any without regular and correct record by the) Tj T*
(comptroller. And though he issued bonds perhaps in excess, he issued none) Tj T*
(without proper registration in the comptroller's records.[356] His) Tj T*
(apparent efforts to conceal facts do not prove fraud; a sufficient motive) Tj T*
(would be furnished by desire to conceal the extravagance of his) Tj T*
(administration. Furthermore, he has been positively acquitted of the) Tj T*
(charge of fraud. In 1878 he returned to Georgia, and the courts proceeded) Tj T*
(to give him "a speedy and public trial." Of his many alleged crimes,) Tj T*
(indictments were secured for three. One indictment was quashed.[357] Upon) Tj T*
(the other two the verdict was "not guilty."[358] His resignation was) Tj T*
(explained in a letter to his "political friends," published on October 31,) Tj T*
(1871.[359] He said that he had obtained evidence of a concerted design) Tj T*
(among certain prominent members of the incoming legislature to impeach) Tj T*
(him \(as they could easily do, with the immense Conservative majority\), and) Tj T*
(instal as governor the Conservative who would be elected president of the) Tj T*
(senate. To resign and put the governorship in the hands of a Republican) Tj T*
(who could not be impeached was the only way to defeat this "nefarious) Tj T*
(scheme." This explanation was of course ignored by Bullock's enemies when) Tj T*
