(it was made; but in view of the lack of evidence that he was guilty of any) Tj T*
(fraud, and in view of the positive evidence to the contrary, there is now) Tj T*
(no reason to doubt it.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The governor made extraordinary use of the pardoning power. According to a) Tj T*
(statement sanctioned by him, he pardoned four hundred and ninety-eight) Tj T*
(criminals, forty-one of whom were convicted or accused of murder,) Tj T*
(fifty-two of burglary, five of arson, and eight of robbery.[360] The) Tj T*
(leader of the Conservative party at that time, B. H. Hill, emphatically) Tj T*
(declared in a public statement that the governor had no worse motive than) Tj T*
("kindness of heart."[361]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(To sum up the case against the reconstruction government, we have seen) Tj T*
(that it was extravagant, that it mismanaged the state railroad, and that) Tj T*
(it pardoned a great many criminals. It was not guilty of the enormities) Tj T*
(often associated with reconstruction; but it was a government composed of) Tj T*
(men who obtained political position only through the interference of an) Tj T*
(outside power--it was the product of a system conceived partly in) Tj T*
(vengeance, partly in folly, and partly in political strategy, and imposed) Tj T*
(by force. It was hated partly for what it did, but more for what it was.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CHAPTER X) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(CONCLUSION) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A Confederate veteran recently remarked amid great applause at an assembly) Tj T*
(in Atlanta that there never was a conqueror so magnanimous as the North,) Tj T*
(for within six years from the surrender of the southern armies she had) Tj T*
(allowed the South to take part in her national councils. Nevertheless,) Tj T*
(within those six years the Congressional Disciplinarians gave the South a) Tj T*
(discipline which she will never forget. It did not result in permanent) Tj T*
(estrangement between the North and the South, for sectional bitterness) Tj T*
(seems extinct. But whether there was any profit in it--whether, in case) Tj T*
(the South never again attempts to secede, that happy omission will be due) Tj T*
(to reconstruction--may be doubted.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Was there a clearer gain from the humanitarian point of view? We have seen) Tj T*
(that at the close of the war a spirit of gratitude and philanthropy) Tj T*
(prevailed among the most influential of the southern white people as) Tj T*
(regards the negroes. Instead of allowing this spirit to develop and in the) Tj T*
(course of time to produce its natural results, the North, believing that) Tj T*
(suffrage was essential to the negro's welfare and progress, forced the) Tj T*
(South to enfranchise him, by reconstruction. This caused the negro untold) Tj T*
(immediate harm \(since reconstruction was a contributary cause of) Tj T*
(Kukluxism\), and delayed his ultimate advance by giving the friendly spirit) Tj T*
(of the white people a check in its development from which it has not yet) Tj T*
(recovered.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
