(From the point of view of the Republican Politicians, reconstruction at) Tj T*
(first succeeded, but later proved a mistaken policy. By it they lost the) Tj T*
(support of the southern white men who had been opposed to secession. These) Tj T*
(formed a large party in Georgia. The victory of the federal arms had the) Tj T*
(nature of a party victory for them. They would have added their strength) Tj T*
(to the Republican party. Reconstruction, with its threat of negro) Tj T*
(domination, drove them into the Democratic party, where they still remain.) Tj T*
(For a time this loss was made good by negro votes, but not long.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Without reconstruction there would have been no Fifteenth Amendment. But) Tj T*
(the good will and philanthropy of the people among whom the negro lives,) Tj T*
(which reconstruction took away, would have brought him more benefit than) Tj T*
(the Fifteenth Amendment. Without reconstruction there would have been no) Tj T*
(Fourteenth Amendment. But a long line of decisions of the Supreme Court) Tj T*
(has determined that the Fourteenth Amendment did not achieve the) Tj T*
(nationalization of civil rights--an end which might justify reconstruction) Tj T*
(as a means. In short, reconstruction seems to have produced bad) Tj T*
(government, political rancor, and social violence and disorder, without) Tj T*
(compensating good.) Tj T*
