(a law of Sept. 15, 1870 \(S. L., 1870, p. 10\), authorizing the governor to) Tj T*
(issue bonds for various purposes without specified limit as to amount. The) Tj T*
(rest were issued under an act of Oct. 17, 1870 \(omitted from the session) Tj T*
(laws, see Conley's message just cited\), authorizing the governor to issue) Tj T*
(to the Brunswick and Albany railroad state bonds to the amount of) Tj T*
($1,880,000 in exchange for bonds of the railroad to the amount of) Tj T*
($2,350,000.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In addition to the bonds already mentioned, bonds to the amount of) Tj T*
($600,000 were issued under acts of 1868 \(S. L., 1868, pp. 14 and 138.\)) Tj T*
(These were not sold and were returned to the possession of the state) Tj T*
(during Bullock's administration \(Angier's statement, K. K. R., vol. 6, p.) Tj T*
(162\). Also, before the issue of $3,000,000 mentioned, bonds to the amount) Tj T*
(of $2,000,000 were issued \(Conley's message cited\). These were) Tj T*
(hypothecated with several bankers in New York. Some of them, amounting to) Tj T*
($500,000, were returned and cancelled during Bullock's administration) Tj T*
(\(Conley's message\). The rest, amounting to $1,500,000, remained in the) Tj T*
(hands of the bankers. Conley stated, in January, 1872 \(message cited\),) Tj T*
(that these bonds had been replaced by bonds of a later issue and canceled) Tj T*
(during Bullock's administration, and had therefore ceased to be a claim) Tj T*
(against the state. This statement conflicts with three facts. 1. The) Tj T*
(bankers who held these bonds refused to return them after their alleged) Tj T*
(cancellation. 2. One of these bankers sold the bonds which he held after) Tj T*
(their alleged cancellation \(Henry Clews, _Twenty-eight Years in Wall) Tj T*
(Street_, p. 277\). 3. The legislature of Georgia repudiated these bonds in) Tj T*
(1872, which would have been unnecessary if they had been cancelled. It) Tj T*
(seems probable, therefore, though not certain, that this $1,500,000 should) Tj T*
(be added to the debt incurred by the reconstruction government.) Tj T*
