([355] In the latter part of 1868 and in 1869 the governor paid to a) Tj T*
(certain H. I. Kimball $54,500 from the treasury. He paid this to be used) Tj T*
(in furnishing a building which was at that time occupied as the state) Tj T*
(capital. \(Bullock's statement, B. A., p. 29.\) There was no law authorizing) Tj T*
(this payment, nor was the state under any obligation to make it. The state) Tj T*
(bought the building in 1870 by an act of the legislature which provided) Tj T*
(that the $54,500 should be counted as part of the price. Thus Bullock's) Tj T*
(advance was ratified by the state. \(S. L., 1870, p. 494.\) This, however,) Tj T*
(does not change the character of the act.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
([356] See C. R., April, 1871, and April, 1872. Bullock was accused of) Tj T*
(indorsing the bonds of three railroads contrary to law. In the case of two) Tj T*
(of these \(the Cartersville and Van Wert, or Cherokee railroad, and the) Tj T*
(Bainbridge, Cuthbert and Columbus railroad\) he refuted the charge beyond) Tj T*
(contradiction in his address to the public of 1872. In the case of the) Tj T*
(third \(the Brunswick and Albany railroad\) he admitted that he had indorsed) Tj T*
(bonds before the road had complied with the conditions required by law,) Tj T*
(but said that he did it for the public good. \(B. A., pp. 39-41.\)) Tj T*
